  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nEarly morning radar trends continue to show showers streaming\nthrough the area amid a sluggish cold frontal passage. 06Z\nfrontal analysis places the boundary two-thirds of the way\nthrough the forecast area, having just slipped southeast of the\nCharleston metro and completing its passage down into southeast\nWest Virginia. Behind the front, stratiform rain and heavy low\nto mid-level cloud cover festers. \n\nForecast soundings denote a strong overrunning signature for \nmost of the lowlands and mountains in the post-frontal \nenvironment today, yielding blanketed overcast skies amid \npassing showers. As the boundary slowly marches down the \nmountains and into Virginia/North Carolina, precipitation \nchances will decrease from northwest to southeast. By peak \nheating hours, POPs greater than 50 percent will be capped over \nthe higher terrain, while chances drastically decrease further\nwest into the lower elevations. Some partial clearing could be \nachieved in our far northwestern zones of Perry County late this\nafternoon, who will relish the most influence from high \npressure parked over the Great Lakes region.\n\nToday's setup will yield various temperature spreads in the\nforecast area. Starting in the southern coalfields, the slow\nprogression of the cold front will keep many of these areas in\nthe warm sector for this afternoon, allowing for daytime highs\nto topple into the low 70s. This will also be the area that\ncould observe a rumble of thunder within precipitation this\nafternoon. For the bulk of the lowlands, deep overrunning and\nlingering light showers/drizzle amid post-frontal cold air\nadvection will impose afternoon temperatures hovering in the low\nto potentially mid 60s. In the event of clearing skies in some\nof our southeast Ohio counties today, daytime temperatures may\nsneak a few degrees warmer into the mid to upper 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn encore of the nuisance frontal boundary will take shape for\nthe holiday weekend, advertising dreary and unsettled weather\nfor the upcoming days. An advancing low pressure system will\nlift the stalled boundary back northward as a warm front and\nremaining nestled in close proximity to the forecast area\nthrough at least Memorial Day. As a result, widespread chances\nfor daily showers and thunderstorms will triumph over the\nCentral Appalachians. Little to no reprieve from the rain is\nanticipated heading into next week due to a secondary frontal\nboundary becoming hung up over the forecast area. Rainfall\ntrends will need to be closely monitored through the course of\nthe weekend, as soil conditions could become overwhelmed by\nheavier downpours and/or repetitive rounds of rain, leading to\nincreasing risk for higher water issues.\n\nAmid the unsettled weather pattern for the holiday weekend, a \nwarming trend commences with the northward progression of the\nfront. A 10 to 15 degree rise in daytime temperatures will be \nnoted from Thursday/Friday into Sunday and Memorial Day. Despite\nthe warm up, instability will struggle to take shape this \nweekend, and therefore the risk for severe weather will be low.",
